/* SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
 *
 * ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1 — flash size only.
 *
 * Console: **left as the board devicetree sets it**, i.e. `uart0` on the
 * board's UART port (the one marked UART, not the one marked USB). That port
 * carries a USB-serial bridge with DTR/RTS wired to EN/BOOT, so esptool can
 * reset the board into download mode on its own — no BOOT/RESET button press
 * to flash, and no losing the first lines of boot output to a USB re-enumeration.
 * That makes it the better default for this board, so this overlay deliberately
 * does *not* re-route the console onto USB-Serial-JTAG the way the ESP32-C6
 * overlay does.
 *
 * If you would rather use the native USB port, add:
 *
 *     &usb_serial { status = "okay"; };
 *     &usb_otg    { status = "disabled"; };   // shares GPIO19/20 with the above
 *     / { chosen {
 *             zephyr,console   = &usb_serial;
 *             zephyr,shell-uart = &usb_serial;
 *     }; };
 *
 * and be aware that a board running unrelated factory firmware on that port may
 * refuse every esptool reset strategy until you hold BOOT and tap RESET once.
 *
 * Flash size: the board devicetree includes `esp32s3_wroom_n8.dtsi`, which
 * declares an 8 MB part. The board in hand is an **N16R8** — `esptool flash-id`
 * reports 16 MB (manufacturer 0x1c, device 0x7118) on an ESP32-S3 rev v0.2 with
 * MAC 7c:0c:5f:5a:6e:b8, plus 8 MB of octal PSRAM that this firmware does not
 * use. Declare the real size. The partition table
 * (`partitions_0x0_amp_4M.dtsi`) lives entirely in the low 4 MB either way, so
 * this changes no layout; it just stops anything reasoning from `flash0` being
 * wrong about the part. On a genuine N8 board, drop this block.
 *
 * No `&wifi` node is enabled here: unlike the QT Py S3 and the S2 Feather, the
 * ESP32-S3-DevKitC board devicetree already sets it to "okay".
 */

&flash0 {
	reg = <0x0 DT_SIZE_M(16)>;
};
